\description{
The conditional residual \verb{e = y - X b - Z u} is computed by the engine at the final
components, together with the fitted values and, in \code{\link[=model]{model()}}, the leverage \code{h_ii} of
each record in the mixed model equations (Hodges 1998): \verb{h_ii = w_i' C^-1 w_i} with
\code{w_i} the row of \verb{[X Z]} scaled by \code{sqrt(weight)} and \code{C} the coefficient matrix,
read from the selective inverse the PEV already uses, so it costs no extra
factorization. \code{\link[=model_ar1]{model_ar1()}} and \code{\link[=model_mt]{model_mt()}} compute their residuals and \code{resid_var}
with one more assembly, factorization and selective inverse at the final components,
at the end of every fit: the cost of about one more REML iteration, and a second pass
through the peak memory of the fit (if that pass fails, the fit is kept and the
residuals are \code{NA}, with the reason in \code{message}). The variance of the residual is NOT
the residual variance:
\code{Var(e_i) = s2e (1 - h_ii) / w_i}, which is smallest in small contemporary groups. On a
simulated animal model with one record per animal (h2 = 0.4, true components,
\verb{validation/residual_diagnostics_calibration.R alavanca}) it averaged about 0.35 s2e in
groups of two records and 0.69 s2e in groups of forty; the figures move with h2 and
the layout. The raw conditional residual is heteroscedastic by construction, and only
the standardized one has unit variance under the model.
}
\details{
